==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From the novel ''A Clockwork Orange'' (1962) by [[w:Anthony Burgess|Anthony Burgess]], in which the main character is programmed to be incapable of antisocial behavior.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[person]] or [[organism]] with a [[mechanistic]] [[morality]] or lack of [[free will]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1996|author=Mark Dery|title=Escape Velocity
|passage=Contrarily, he may be saying, “Look what your computerized, commodified society has made of me—a '''clockwork orange''', for all appearances organic but mechanical."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author=Patrick Brantlinger|title=The Reading Lesson: The Threat of Mass Literacy in Nineteenth-Century British Fiction
|passage=The telos of the pathologization of crime is the perfected robot or “'''clockwork orange'''” of present-day behaviorism and sociobiology, descendants of eighteenth- and early-nineteenth-century associationists like Jeremy Bentham.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Diederik Aerts|author2=Jan Broekaert|author3=Ernest Mathijs|title=Einstein Meets Magritte: An Interdisciplinary Reflection: The White Book of "Einstein Meets Magritte
|passage=This one took reality to be a large machine, a ‘'''clockwork orange'''', an automaton.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Enoch Brater|title=Arthur Miller's America: Theater and Culture in a Time of Change
|passage=Under the archbishop's ceiling, the self is not a '''clockwork orange''' programmed by the state but something more unnerving: Peer Gynt's onion, layers of performance without a core.}}

